</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Related work</h2>

<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Machine unlearning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Machine unlearning aims to remove the influence of specific data from trained models <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">15</a>]</cite>. While exact unlearning via retraining <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">16</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite> provides theoretical guarantees, its computational cost and data requirements often make it impractical. Consequently, research has focused on developing various approximate unlearning methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, which have shown effectiveness across different domains including classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>, <a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">23</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">25</a>]</cite>, generative tasks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>, <a href="#bib.bib27" title="" class="ltx_ref">27</a>, <a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>, <a href="#bib.bib28" title="" class="ltx_ref">28</a>]</cite>, federated learning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>, <a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">30</a>]</cite>, graph neural networks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">31</a>, <a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">32</a>]</cite>, and recommendation systems <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib33" title="" class="ltx_ref">33</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLM unlearning</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLM unlearning has attracted wide research attention driven by concerns over privacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">5</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">6</a>]</cite>, potential biases <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1</a>, <a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>]</cite>, and misuse <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite>. Dominant approaches include <em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">optimization-based methods</em> that fine-tune model parameters for unlearning. Early algorithms like Gradient Ascent (GA) maximize loss on forget data to promote forgetting <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">4</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite>, but this unbounded objective can lead to model degradation. Preference optimization-based methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>, <a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> have been proposed as a solution to this issue. Additionally, some research also explore second-order optimization for unlearning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">36</a>]</cite>.
<em id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Other strategies</em> operate beyond direct parameter updates, such as using auxiliary models to isolate or counteract the knowledge targeted for removal <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">37</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> or data manipulation techniques like substituting target responses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>, <a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">3</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>, <a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite>. Training-free methods using instructions have also emerged <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>, <a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">44</a>]</cite>. However, results from recent benchmarks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>, <a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite> suggest that instability inherent in many algorithms can cause either under-forgetting or over-forgetting.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Preference optimization</h5>

<div id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Aligning LLMs with human value is traditionally approached through Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, a multi-stage process involving supervised fine-tuning, reward model training, and reinforcement learning optimization.
Its complexity motivates the development of DPO (Direct Preference Optimization) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, which reformulates the RLHF objective for direct policy updates from preference data, bypassing explicit reward modeling. Subsequent work has extended this paradigm <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">47</a>, <a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">48</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">49</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">50</a>]</cite>. Notably, Token-level Direct Preference Optimization (TDPO) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> introduces granular control by operating at the token-level. Our algorithm derivation draws inspiration from this method.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>LLM unlearning problem formulation</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The LLM unlearning task, while varied in formulation, typically involves a forget set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, a retain set <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>, and an initial LLM <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>. The objective is to update <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> to a new model <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> that eliminates knowledge specific to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> while preserving performance on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math>. Optimization-based methods typically achieve this by minimizing a combined loss:</p>
<table id="S3.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{f}(\theta)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{r}(\theta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}_{f}(\theta)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{r}(\theta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> encourages knowledge preservation, <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{f}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{f}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math> promotes forgetting information related to <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>λ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda</annotation></semantics></math> is a hyperparameter controlling the retain strength. Different unlearning methods employ varying losses: for instance, Gradient Ascent (GA) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> promotes forgetting by minimizing the likelihood on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{f}(\theta)=\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{f}(\theta)=\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>), while Gradient Difference (GradDiff) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite> combines this with reverse objective on <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{r}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{r}</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{r}(\theta)=-\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>r</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{r}(\theta)=-\log\pi_{\theta}(y|x)</annotation></semantics></math>), details in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Baseline Methods ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>From preference optimization to unlearning</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Direct Preference Optimization (DPO)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The primary contribution of DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite> is simplifying the training process of Reinforcement Learning from Human Feedback (RLHF) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">45</a>]</cite>, the previously dominant fine-tuning method. Specifically, given a reference policy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math> (often the model after supervised fine-tuning), <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> represents the model undergoing RL fine-tuning, initialized with <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}=\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}=\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>. The RLHF optimization objective is:</p>
<table id="S3.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\{\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},y\sim\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}[r(x,y)]-\beta D_{KL}[\pi_{\theta}(y|x)||\pi_{ref}(y|x)]\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\{\mathbb{E}_{x\sim\mathcal{D},y\sim\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}[r(x,y)]-\beta D_{KL}[\pi_{\theta}(y|x)||\pi_{ref}(y|x)]\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}</annotation></semantics></math> is the dataset, <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> represents the reward, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>β</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta</annotation></semantics></math> is a parameter controlling the deviation from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{ref}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{ref}</annotation></semantics></math>. DPO finds that <a href="#S3.E2" title="In Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) ‣ 3.2 From preference optimization to unlearning ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> has a theoretical solution for the optimal policy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}(y|x)=\frac{\pi_{ref}(y|x)e^{r(x,y)/\beta}}{Z(x)},\quad\text{where }Z(x)=\sum_{y}\pi_{ref}(y|x)e^{r(x,y)/\beta}\text{.}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>π</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>where </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mi>y</mi></munder><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>β</mi></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}(y|x)=\frac{\pi_{ref}(y|x)e^{r(x,y)/\beta}}{Z(x)},\quad\text{where }Z(x)=\sum_{y}\pi_{ref}(y|x)e^{r(x,y)/\beta}\text{.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S3.E3" title="In Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) ‣ 3.2 From preference optimization to unlearning ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> establishes a mapping between the reward function and the optimal policy. To align with human preferences, DPO utilizes the Bradley-Terry (BT) model to model preference pairs and subsequently derives the final optimization objective function:</p>
<table id="S3.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_{w}|x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{l}|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_{l}|x)}\right)\right]\right\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\left\{\mathbb{E}_{(x,y_{w},y_{l})\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\log\sigma\left(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_{w}|x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{l}|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y_{l}|x)}\right)\right]\right\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Negative Preference Optimization (NPO)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">NPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> adapts <a href="#S3.E4" title="In Direct Preference Optimization (DPO) ‣ 3.2 From preference optimization to unlearning ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for unlearning by omitting the preferred response <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{w}</annotation></semantics></math> terms, thus focusing solely on penalizing undesired ‘forget’ responses <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> (treating as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>) over <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{D}_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{D}_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. NPO uses the same retain loss like GradDiff method in <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 LLM unlearning problem formulation ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. Following the formulation presented in the original paper, the resulting forget loss term is:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{NPO-f}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mrow><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>O</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{NPO-f}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\frac{2}{\beta}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim D_{f}}\left[\log\sigma\left(-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y|x)}\right)\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>[</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\frac{2}{\beta}\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim D_{f}}\left[\log\sigma\left(-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y|x)}{\pi_{ref}(y|x)}\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Token-level Direct Preference Optimization (TDPO)</h5>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">TDPO models text-generation as a Markov Decision Process <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite>, where state <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s_{t}=[x,y^{&lt;t}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s_{t}=[x,y^{&lt;t}]</annotation></semantics></math> consists of the prompt and previously generated tokens, and action <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> corresponds to selecting the next token <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y^{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>t</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y^{t}</annotation></semantics></math>. Accordingly, unlike DPO’s response-level optimization, TDPO defines rewards and proposes an objective function at the token-level:</p>
<table id="S3.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{x,y^{&lt;t}\sim\mathcal{D},z\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-\beta D_{KL}(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi>max</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub></munder><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\max_{\pi_{\theta}}\mathbb{E}_{x,y^{&lt;t}\sim\mathcal{D},z\sim\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-\beta D_{KL}(\pi_{\theta}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the advantage function, analogous to the implicit reward function <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> in DPO, quantifying <em id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the preference for selecting token <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">z</annotation></semantics></math></em> in the given context. Similar to DPO, TDPO derives a closed-form solution for the optimal policy <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi^{*}_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi^{*}_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}^{*}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])=\frac{\pi_{\text{ref}}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])\exp(\frac{1}{\beta}Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z))}{Z([x,y^{&lt;t}];\beta)}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}^{*}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])=\frac{\pi_{\text{ref}}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])\exp(\frac{1}{\beta}Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z))}{Z([x,y^{&lt;t}];\beta)},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.3" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="Z([x,y^{&lt;t}];\beta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}e^{\frac{1}{\beta}Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>;</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>β</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z([x,y^{&lt;t}];\beta)=\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}e^{\frac{1}{\beta}Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)}</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is state-action function related to <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-V_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-V_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}])</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E8.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.4" class="ltx_p">TDPO also employs the BT model and derives its final loss function, where one variant is given by:</p>
<table id="S3.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{TDPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{\text{ref}})=-\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\sigma\bigg(\bigg(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{w}|x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{l}|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{l}|x)}\bigg)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>TDPO</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{TDPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{\text{ref}})=-\mathbb{E}\bigg[\log\sigma\bigg(\bigg(\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{w}|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{w}|x)}-\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}(y_{l}|x)}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(y_{l}|x)}\bigg)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E9.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle-\bigg(\beta D_{{SeqKL}}(x,y_{l};\pi_{\text{ref}}||\pi_{\theta})-\beta D_{{SeqKL}}(x,y_{w};\pi_{\text{ref}}||\pi_{\theta})\bigg)\bigg)\bigg]," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle-\bigg(\beta D_{{SeqKL}}(x,y_{l};\pi_{\text{ref}}||\pi_{\theta})-\beta D_{{SeqKL}}(x,y_{w};\pi_{\text{ref}}||\pi_{\theta})\bigg)\bigg)\bigg],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.5" class="ltx_p">where</p>
<table id="S3.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{1}||\pi_{2})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}D_{KL}(\pi_{1}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{2}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">=</mo><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{1}||\pi_{2})=\sum_{t=1}^{T}D_{KL}(\pi_{1}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{2}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Method</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we first derive the DiPO algorithm in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>, then analyze its gradient in <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 DiPO gradient analysis ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>, and finally detail the construction of these preference pairs and the final unlearning objective in <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Preference Pair Construction and Final Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO)</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach stems from the formulation of text generation as a Markov Decision Process (MDP) in TDPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">51</a>]</cite> and utilizes its closed-form solution for the optimal policy detailed in <a href="#S3.E7" title="In Token-level Direct Preference Optimization (TDPO) ‣ 3.2 From preference optimization to unlearning ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. We can rearrange to solve for <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)=\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}^{*}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}+\beta\log Z([x,y^{&lt;t}];\beta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>β</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)=\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}^{*}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}+\beta\log Z([x,y^{&lt;t}];\beta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Denoting the advantage function <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)</annotation></semantics></math> as <math id="S4.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)</annotation></semantics></math>, which represents <em id="S4.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">the immediate reward per step</em> in the context of RL. According to <a href="#S3.E8" title="In Token-level Direct Preference Optimization (TDPO) ‣ 3.2 From preference optimization to unlearning ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, we can derive the expression as:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex3.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)-\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[Q_{\pi_{\text{ref}}}([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E12.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}^{*}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}+\beta D_{KL}(\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\theta}^{*}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><mi>log</mi><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\beta\log\frac{\pi_{\theta}^{*}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}{\pi_{\text{ref}}(z|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}+\beta D_{KL}(\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\theta}^{*}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the token-level immediate reward <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)</annotation></semantics></math>, the distribution-level immediate reward <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\pi}(x,y^{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi}(x,y^{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math> at step <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> under a distribution <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as its expectation:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="r_{\pi}(x,y^{&lt;t})\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)]," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>π</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>r</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi}(x,y^{&lt;t})\coloneqq\mathbb{E}_{z\sim\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])}[r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)],</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmdefinition1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)</annotation></semantics></math> can be expanded using <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> to yield:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle r_{\pi}(x,y^{&lt;t})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle r_{\pi}(x,y^{&lt;t})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\beta D_{KL}(\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))-\beta D_{KL}(\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\theta}^{*}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>π</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\beta D_{KL}(\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))-\beta D_{KL}(\pi(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\theta}^{*}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\quad+\beta D_{KL}(\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\theta}^{*}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}]))." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\quad+\beta D_{KL}(\pi_{\text{ref}}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])||\pi_{\theta}^{*}(\cdot|[x,y^{&lt;t}])).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a discount factor <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math>, the distribution-level return <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\pi}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\pi}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> for a complete trajectory <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y</annotation></semantics></math> (i.e. response) under distribution <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>π</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi</annotation></semantics></math> is the discounted sum of <math id="S4.Thmdefinition2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r_{\pi}([x,y^{&lt;t}])" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r_{\pi}([x,y^{&lt;t}])</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\pi}(x,y)\coloneqq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t-1}r_{\pi}([x,y^{&lt;t}])." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.111em">≔</mo><mrow><munderover><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>T</mi></munderover><mrow><msup><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\pi}(x,y)\coloneqq\sum_{t=1}^{T}\gamma^{t-1}r_{\pi}([x,y^{&lt;t}]).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we set the discount factor <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Substituting the expression for <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r([x,y^{&lt;t}],z)</annotation></semantics></math> in <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> and using the definition of Sequence KL divergence in <a href="#S3.E10" title="In Token-level Direct Preference Optimization (TDPO) ‣ 3.2 From preference optimization to unlearning ‣ 3 Preliminaries ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, the return <math id="S4.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R_{\pi}(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\pi}(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math> can be rewritten to the final form:</p>
<table id="S4.E13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="R_{\pi}(x,y)=\beta D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi||\pi_{\text{ref}})-\beta D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi||\pi_{\theta}^{*})+\beta D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{\text{ref}}||\pi_{\theta}^{*})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>R</mi><mi>π</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><mi>π</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msubsup><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi><mo>∗</mo></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R_{\pi}(x,y)=\beta D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi||\pi_{\text{ref}})-\beta D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi||\pi_{\theta}^{*})+\beta D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{\text{ref}}||\pi_{\theta}^{*}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">We refer readers to <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Distribution-level Return Derivation ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Details ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> for a complete derivation.
Consistent with DPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, we also model preferences using the Bradley-Terry (BT) model. From this, we derive the final loss function for DiPO, which is summarized in the following theorem:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(DiPO Loss Function)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given the expression for the token-level immediate reward in <a href="#S4.E12" title="In 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, under the <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition1" title="Definition 4.1. ‣ 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> and <a href="#S4.Thmdefinition2" title="Definition 4.2. ‣ 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Definition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> (with discount factor <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma=1</annotation></semantics></math>), and applying the Bradley-Terry method to model preference pairs, the DiPO loss function is given by:</span></p>
<table id="S4.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DiPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{w},\pi_{l},\pi_{\text{ref}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">DiPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{\text{DiPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{w},\pi_{l},\pi_{\text{ref}})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\log\sigma\left(\beta\Big(D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\theta})-D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\theta})\Big)\right.\right." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><mo lspace="0em">−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo>[</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mo>(</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}_{(x,y)\sim\mathcal{D}}\left[\log\sigma\left(\beta\Big(D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\theta})-D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\theta})\Big)\right.\right.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\qquad\left.\left.+\beta\Big(D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\text{ref}})-D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\text{ref}})\Big)\right)\right]." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>β</mi><mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">(</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext class="ltx_mathvariant_italic">ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="1.600em" minsize="1.600em">)</mo></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow><mo>]</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\qquad\left.\left.+\beta\Big(D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\text{ref}})-D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\text{ref}})\Big)\right)\right].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The detailed proof is provided in <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Detailed proof of DiPO loss ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Details ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>DiPO gradient analysis</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To analyze the gradient dynamics, we can simplify the loss expression in <a href="#S4.E14" title="In Theorem 4.1 (DiPO Loss Function). ‣ 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> further. We introduce the following shorthand notations for a given sample <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E15.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\theta}),\quad x_{2}\coloneqq D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\theta})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\theta}),\quad x_{2}\coloneqq D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\theta}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle C</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E16.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\coloneqq D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\text{ref}})-D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\text{ref}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>≔</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>;</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\coloneqq D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{w}||\pi_{\text{ref}})-D_{SeqKL}(x,y;\pi_{l}||\pi_{\text{ref}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> depend on the trainable policy <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math>, while <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is treated as a constant with respect to the parameters <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> of the policy <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> during optimization.
Substituting these into the loss function <a href="#S4.E14" title="In Theorem 4.1 (DiPO Loss Function). ‣ 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>, and considering a single term in the summation for a specific sample <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(x,y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(x,y)</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S4.E17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L=-\log\sigma\left(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L=-\log\sigma\left(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">We compute the partial derivatives of <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>L</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L</annotation></semantics></math> with respect to <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Using the chain rule and the fact that <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{\prime}(z)=\sigma(z)(1-\sigma(z))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>σ</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>z</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{\prime}(z)=\sigma(z)(1-\sigma(z))</annotation></semantics></math>, we have:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{1}}=-\beta\left(1-\sigma(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C))\right),\quad\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{2}}=\beta\left(1-\sigma(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C))\right)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{1}}=-\beta\left(1-\sigma(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C))\right),\quad\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{2}}=\beta\left(1-\sigma(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C))\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.4" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma(\cdot)\in(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma(\cdot)\in(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math>, the term <math id="S4.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(1-\sigma(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C)))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(1-\sigma(\beta(x_{1}-x_{2}+C)))</annotation></semantics></math> is always positive. This leads to the following optimization dynamics:</p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{1}}&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{1}}&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, minimizing <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> via gradient descent increases <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{1}=D_{SeqKL}(\pi_{l}||\pi_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{1}=D_{SeqKL}(\pi_{l}||\pi_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, effectively pushing the distribution <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">away</span> from the dispreferred distribution <math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{l}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{l}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Conversely, since <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{2}}&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mo>∂</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><mrow><mo>∂</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mfrac><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{\partial L}{\partial x_{2}}&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, minimizing <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}</annotation></semantics></math> decreases <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="x_{2}=D_{SeqKL}(\pi_{w}||\pi_{\theta})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>K</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x_{2}=D_{SeqKL}(\pi_{w}||\pi_{\theta})</annotation></semantics></math>, thereby pulling the distribution <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{\theta}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{\theta}</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">closer</span> to the preferred distribution <math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{w}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{w}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Preference Pair Construction and Final Objective</h3>

<figure id="S4.F1" class="ltx_figure ltx_align_floatright" style="width:46%;"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2510.04773v1/filter.svg" id="S4.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:215/155;" width="215" height="155"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 1: </span>Construction of memory-enhancing distribution <math id="S4.F1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and forgetting-promoting distribution <math id="S4.F1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> by a memory vector filtered from origin logits.
</figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our approach to constructing preference pairs <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{w},\pi_{l})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{w},\pi_{l})</annotation></semantics></math> from the model’s logits <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> focuses on modulating a small subset of high-probability tokens: If these tokens correspond to undesirable information, suppressing their logits naturally steers the model towards alternative, non-sensitive outputs; Conversely, if the high-probability tokens are unrelated to the sensitive information, suppressing this small fraction is unlikely to directly promote undesirable outputs due to the vastness of the vocabulary table. This inherent safety allow us to employ a straightforward filtering mechanism.
Specifically, we first identify a ‘memory vector’ <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> by isolating the logits of high-confidence tokens (e.g., top 5% identified via top-k filtering from <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{z}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{z}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math>), setting all other token logits in <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbf{m}_{t}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbf{m}_{t}</annotation></semantics></math> to zero. Then we can construct the memory-enhancing distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> and the forgetting-promoting distribution <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}</annotation></semantics></math> by adding or subtracting this memory vector, scaled by a factor <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>α</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E19.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\pi_{m}(\cdot|x,y^{&lt;t})=\text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{t}+\alpha\mathbf{m}_{t}),\quad\pi_{f}(\cdot|x,y^{&lt;t})=\text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{t}-\alpha\mathbf{m}_{t})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>softmax</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋅</mo><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mtext>softmax</mtext><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝐳</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mi>α</mi><msub><mi>𝐦</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\pi_{m}(\cdot|x,y^{&lt;t})=\text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{t}+\alpha\mathbf{m}_{t}),\quad\pi_{f}(\cdot|x,y^{&lt;t})=\text{softmax}(\mathbf{z}_{t}-\alpha\mathbf{m}_{t}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(19)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><a href="#S4.F1" title="In 4.3 Preference Pair Construction and Final Objective ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Figure</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> illustrates this mechanism, showing how adding or subtracting the memory vector shapes the distribution towards memorization <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{m}</annotation></semantics></math> or forgetting <math id="S4.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\pi_{f}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\pi_{f}</annotation></semantics></math>. More details are provided in <a href="#A4.SS2" title="D.2 Details on Filter mechanism ‣ Appendix D Experiments Details ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">D.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Crucially, the same pair <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\pi_{m},\pi_{f})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\pi_{m},\pi_{f})</annotation></semantics></math> derived from the model’s logits can be utilized for both the forget and retain objectives by simply reversing their roles in preference pairs. This yields the forget objective <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-f}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO-f</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-f}}}</annotation></semantics></math> and retain objective <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-r}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO-r</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-r}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, formulated based on the DiPO loss <a href="#S4.E14" title="In Theorem 4.1 (DiPO Loss Function). ‣ 4.1 Derivation of Distribution Preference Optimization (DiPO) ‣ 4 Method ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Equation</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>:</p>
<table id="S4.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S4.E20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-f}}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO-f</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-f}}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E20.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DiPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{w}=\pi_{f},\pi_{l}=\pi_{m},\pi_{\text{ref}})," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DiPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{w}=\pi_{f},\pi_{l}=\pi_{m},\pi_{\text{ref}}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(20)</span></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S4.E21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-r}}}(\theta)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO-r</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-r}}}(\theta)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S4.E21.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DiPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{w}=\pi_{m},\pi_{l}=\pi_{f},\pi_{\text{ref}})." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>θ</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>l</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mi>f</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>π</mi><mtext>ref</mtext></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathcal{L}_{\text{DiPO}}(\pi_{\theta};\pi_{w}=\pi_{m},\pi_{l}=\pi_{f},\pi_{\text{ref}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(21)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The final optimization objective for unlearning then combines these components:</p>
<table id="S4.E22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\min_{\theta}\left(\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-f}}}(\theta)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-r}}}(\theta)\right)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder><mi>min</mi><mi>θ</mi></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO-f</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mtext>DiPO-r</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>θ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\min_{\theta}\mathcal{L}(\theta)=\min_{\theta}\left(\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-f}}}(\theta)+\lambda\mathcal{L}_{{\text{DiPO-r}}}(\theta)\right).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(22)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Following the common practice in optimization-based unlearning approaches, we set the hyperparameter <math id="S4.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda=1</annotation></semantics></math> in DiPO. We provided the pseudo-code in <a href="#A2.SS3" title="B.3 Pseudo-code of DiPO ‣ Appendix B Theoretical Details ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Experiments</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare our proposed DiPO algorithm with baseline unlearning methods across two widely used benchmarks: TOFU <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">13</a>]</cite>, focusing on forgetting knowledge of fictitious authors, and MUSE <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">14</a>]</cite>, targeting the removal of copyrighted content. We refer to the initial model before unlearning as the “Original” model, while the model retrained from scratch after removing the forget-set data as the “Retrain” model.
This section presents the main experimental results for TOFU (<a href="#S5.SS1" title="5.1 Experiments on TOFU ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a>) and MUSE (<a href="#S5.SS2" title="5.2 Experiments on MUSE ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.2</span></a>), followed by further analyses and ablation studies of DiPO in <a href="#S5.SS3" title="5.3 Additional analysis ‣ 5 Experiments ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baseline Methods</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compare DiPO against several optimization-based baselines, including GA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>, GradDiff <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">2</a>, <a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">10</a>]</cite> and NPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite>. For TOFU, we also incorporate other advanced unlearning framework such as ULD <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">38</a>]</cite> (we use the results from its original paper) and AltPO <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">35</a>]</cite> for a broader comparison. Detailed descriptions of all baseline methods are provided in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Baseline Methods ‣ Distribution Preference Optimization: A Fine-grained Perspective for LLM Unlearning" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S5.F2" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2510.04773v1/tofu-main.png" id="S5.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:471/142;" width="471" height="142" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure">Figure 2: </span>Performance analysis on TOFU at the best epoch over five seeds. (a) FQ vs. MU on TOFU-5% and TOFU-10%. DiPO achieves the best trade-off (closest to the “Retrain” target). (b) Training curves of FQ and MU on TOFU-10%, showcasing DiPO’s stability and efficacy.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Experiments on TOFU</h3>

<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table ltx_align_floatright" style="width:50%;">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 2: </span>The best-epoch performance averaged over five seeds on TOFU benchmark. Scores closer to “Retrain” are better. <span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Bold</span> indicates best results among all methods.</figcaption>
<table id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S5.T2.4.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Method</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">TOFU-1%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">TOFU-5%</span></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.1.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S5.T2.4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">TOFU-10%</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.4.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.4.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><em id="S5.T2.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">FQ</em></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><em id="S5.T2.4.2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">MU</em></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><em id="S5.T2.4.2.3.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">FQ</em></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.2.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><em id="S5.T2.4.2.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">MU</em></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.2.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><em id="S5.T2.4.2.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">FQ</em></td>
<td id="S5.T2.4.2.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><em id="S5.T2.4.2.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic" style="font-size:90%;">MU</em></td></tr>
<tr id="S5.T2.4.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S5.T2.4.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_r ltx_border_t" style="padding-left:3.0pt;padding-right:3.0pt;"><span id="S5.T2.4.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Original</span></td>
